{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>With a stone or with a fist.</b> A fist is the hand when the fingers are gathered together, and it is the language of the Rabbis, of blessed memory: 'the fist of Ben Avtinas,' 'of the house of David, they were men of fists.' And Scripture mentioned two kinds of blows — one severe and one light that does not usually kill — to say that in both cases an assessment is required, and they confine him (the assailant) if he dies; for instance, if he struck him mortally, he is put to death — he is a murderer. But if he does not die, he pays for idleness (שבת) and healing (ורפוי). And the meaning of the verse is that when the injured party regains strength and walks about regularly outdoors, in the markets and streets, with his staff, as is the way of the weak who have recovered from illness, the striker is cleared. From this they inferred that even if the striker was negligent with his life and he later died of his weakness, he is not put to death. And the verse speaks in the present tense (בהוה), for the injured parties who have taken to bed do not walk outdoors until the blow heals and they emerge from danger. And this is the sense of ״והתהלך בחוץ״ — for if he rises and walks about in the house with his staff, he is not cleared." "<b>And in the Mekhilta.</b> ״אם יקום והתהלך״ — might I think even in his house? It says ״בחוץ״. If outdoors — might I think even a bedridden one (מתנוונה)? It says ״אם יקום״. And this too is very reasonable, that Scripture should say: if he rises completely from his bed and walks about regularly in the streets, so that he does not return to his bed when he comes indoors, as is the way of the bedridden — even though he is weak and leans on a staff — the striker is cleared. And the rule is that all of this is as it were a parable in the present tense (בהוה), and the law is that they must assess him for life. And this is what Onkelos translated: על בורייה. And it says: ״רק שבתו יתן ורפא ירפא״ — and it did not say ״את שבתו ואת רפואתו יתן״, to indicate that he pays the fee to physicians and they heal him, and the injured party cannot demand that he pay the fee while he himself does other things with him; rather, he must heal him in any case. Thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:38.692557865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}